WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.580
Flow depends on the hydraulic system, not a single component rating.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.060
Hydraulic impedance describes how a path resists flow.

3
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:12.420
Equipment, couplings, hoses and manifolds contribute to the configured path, and parallel

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.180
branches can receive different flows under the same overall system conditions.

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.920
Don't use a pump's headline rating or a coupling size as proof

6
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.080
that each equipment branch receives what it needs.

7
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.360
Establish the available conditions at the required boundary and verify the circuit

8
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.240
under the supported equipment population.

9
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.280
Changes to routing or components can change that result.

10
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.260
Keep pressure limits and heat rejection in the same review without treating

11
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.260
them as interchangeable.

12
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.980
Adequate flow doesn't by itself establish an acceptable receiving temperature, and a

13
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.800
thermal rating doesn't prove mechanical pressure compatibility.

14
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.520
Residual air-side heat still needs its own supported path.

15
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.740
The linked thermal-boundaries course gives deeper context; here, retain the complete path

16
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:54.520
and the evidence at each handoff.

